\begin{abstract}
In recent years, significant research has been conducted into classifying application (app) user feedback, primarily relying on supervised machine learning algorithms.
% However, creating large and accurately labeled datasets to fine-tune more generalizable calssifiers, remains an important challenge, often demanding considerable time and resources.
However, fine-tuning more generalizable classifiers based on existing labeled datasets remains a important challenge, as creating large and accurately labeled datasets often requires considerable time and resources.
The emergence of large language models (LLMs), pre-trained on extensive datasets, has demonstrated promising results across a variety of natural language processing (NLP) tasks, including text classification and labeled dataset augmentation. However, the potential of LLMs to help in user feedback analysis remains largely unexplored. In this paper, we evaluate the capabilities of four advanced LLMs, including GPT-3.5-Turbo, GPT-4, Flan-T5, and Llama3-70b, to enhance user feedback classification and address the challenge of the limited labeled dataset. To achieve this, we conduct several experiments on eight datasets that have been meticulously labeled in prior research. These datasets include user reviews from app stores, posts from the X platform, and discussions from the public forums, widely recognized as representative sources of app user feedback. We analyze the performance of various LLMs in identifying both fine-grained and coarse-grained user feedback categories. Given the substantial volume of daily user feedback and the computational limitations of LLMs, we leverage these models as an annotation tool to augment labeled datasets with general and app-specific data. This augmentation aims to enhance the performance of state-of-the-art BERT-based classification models. Our findings indicate that LLMs when guided by well-crafted prompts, can effectively classify user feedback into coarse-grained categories. However, the performance of LLMs in identifying fine-grained categories was less satisfactory. Moreover, augmenting the training dataset with datasets labeled using the consensus of LLMs can significantly enhance classifier performance.
\end{abstract}


% keywords can be removed
\keywords{User Feedback Classification \and Foundation Models \and Intention Mining \and Machine Learning.}


\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}
In recent years, the number of available mobile applications in app stores has rapidly increased~\cite{chen}. As of the second quarter of 2024, approximately 2.26 million apps were available on Google Play~\cite{gplay_stat} and 1.55 million on the App Store~\cite{appstore_stat}. With thousands of similar apps vying for user attention, developers face intense competition to attract and retain users~\cite{noei}. As a result, accurately identifying and understanding user needs and preferences and addressing existing app issues are essential for developers to succeed in this competition~\cite{Gu}.

Users can share and discuss their feedback with each other or with developers through various platforms, such as app stores, forums, issue tracking systems, and social media platforms~\cite{Devin,Nayebi,Tizard,Abedini}. These platforms serve as rich repositories of user requirements, and analyzing their content offers valuable insights that can aid developers in maintaining and improving their apps~\cite{Malgaonkar}. In recent years, substantial research has focused on identifying, classifying, and prioritizing requirements extracted from these sources, yielding promising results~\cite{Iacob,Guzman,Gao,Villarroel,Stanik}. However, many of these approaches, which depend on supervised learning to achieve optimal results, face a critical limitation: ``\emph{the lack of sufficiently labeled datasets}''~\cite{Dabrowski}. The difficulty of the labeling process often results in relatively small and limited labeled datasets~\cite{Dabrowski}. This limitation threatens the generalizability of these approaches and hinders their practical application~\cite{Dabrowski}. Devine et al.~\cite{Devin} highlighted that the available datasets fail to deliver the expected performance in prediction on unseen datasets. Although efforts have been made to address this issue, it remains an open challenge that requires further research and investigation~\cite{Dabrowski}.

In recent years, there has been a surge of interest in LLMs, especially variations of OpenAI's GPT~\cite{openai_models}, which has emerged as one of the most prominent and widely studied models in this domain~\cite{Liu}. These models have provided remarkable accuracy using carefully designed prompts across various NLP-based tasks, including text classification, feature extraction, sentiment analysis, text generation, and data labeling~\cite{Liu,Hou,Ding}. Zhang et al.~\cite{Zhang} demonstrated that GPT-3.5 can effectively classify non-functional requirements and extract features in a zero-shot setting. Their study provides preliminary results using a limited number of datasets. However, it does not comprehensively investigate the potential of LLMs for predicting various categories or identifying optimal prompts for these tasks. To the best of our knowledge, previous studies that have utilized LLMs for labeling tasks and augmenting existing datasets have not specifically focused on the context of mobile app requirements~\cite{Colavito,Ding,Moller,Huang,Aldeen,Arbi}. However, the intriguing results of these studies motivated us to investigate and evaluate the potential of LLMs in improving the accuracy of user feedack classifiers and addressing the suffering challenge of creating sufficient labeled datasets. 
To achieve this, we focus on addressing the following three research questions through empirical studies:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item[\textbf{RQ1}] \emph{Can LLMs be utilized to improve the accuracy of user feedback classification in diverse data sources and classification schemes?}

    \item[\textbf{RQ2}] \emph{Can the establishment of a well-defined coarse-grained classification scheme enhance the effectiveness of LLMs in classifying user feedback?}

    \item[\textbf{RQ3}] \emph{Can LLMs be utilized to augment manually labeled training datasets using general or app-specific data to create lighter and more accurate classification models?}
\end{enumerate}

Accordingly, we first conduct an experiment to evaluate the accuracy of LLMs in the zero-shot setting for classifying user feedback across various classification schemes (RQ1). We incorporate the category definitions provided in the original papers (that introduced these datasets) as part of the input prompts. Our results demonstrate that LLMs achieve better performance in identifying dataset categories when the distinctions between those categories are more pronounced. In fact, using LLMs to identify fine-grained categories in user feedback often yields unsatisfactory results. Therefore, we adapted the datasets to a coarse-grained classification scheme that is more important to developers, including three main categories: \emph{Bug Report}, \emph{Feature Request}, and \emph{Other}. Subsequently, we employed LLMs to classify user feedback based on a new classification scheme in both zero-shot and few-shot settings using a comprehensive prompt (RQ2). Despite the notable improvement in the app store datasets, classifying user feedback in X posts and forum discussions still underperformed.

On the other hand, we face limitations in using LLMs, including high computational resources, associated costs, and restricted request quotas for serverless models like OpenAI variants. Applying these models for classifying user feedback in many apps, especially popular ones with thousands of daily feedback~\cite{Ciurumelea}, can be impractical. Therefore, we aim to create a more accurate and computationally efficient classifier by augmenting the existing human-labeled dataset using the annotating capabilities of LLMs (RQ3). In RQ2, we demonstrated that datasets labeled through the consensus of LLM predictions can achieve high accuracy, specially in classifying app store user feedback. Therefore, we propose utilizing this approach to annotate new general-purpose and app-specific datasets (LLM-labeled datasets).
These datasets can then augment existing manually labeled datasets (human-labeled datasets), creating richer training datasets for fine-tuning transformer-based classification models.
% In RQ2, we demonstrated that the precision of LLMs in classifying app store user feedback can be improved by targeting more distinct and coarse-grained categories within prompts.
Our findings demonstrate that augmenting training dataset can enhance the accuracy and generalizability of fine-tuned classifiers. While this approach does not outperform the accuracy of zero-shot learning, it offers a more practical solution for real-world applications due to the computational constraints and the vast volume of incoming data.

In this paper, we leverage four diverse LLMs, GPT-3.5-turbo~\cite{GPT-3.5}\footnote{Hereafter referred to as GPT-3.5 throughout the paper.}, GPT-4o~\cite{GPT-4o}, Flan-T5~\cite{Flan-t5}, and Llama3-70b~\cite{Llama3}\footnote{Hereafter referred to as Llama3 throughout the paper.}, for our experiments. To evaluate the performance of these LLMs, we use eight user feedback datasets annotated in previous research through appropriate processes. These datasets encompass four from Google Play~\cite{GPlay} and App Store~\cite{AppStore}, two from the X platform\cite{Twitter}, one from the VLC~\cite{VLC} and Firefox~\cite{Tizard} forums, and one from the Reddit~\cite{Reddit} forum. The classification schemes of these datasets vary in terms of labeling policies and levels of granularity.

In general, this paper presents two main contributions:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item We conducted a comprehensive study to evaluate the capabilities of various LLMs in classifying user feedback from diverse sources following different classification schemes. Our findings demonstrate that selecting an appropriate classification scheme can enhance the accuracy of LLMs in identifying different types of user feedback. 

    \item We analyzed how LLMs can be utilized to augment training datasets with general-purpose and app-specific data, leading to improved performance of transformer-based classifiers.

\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Paper Structure} In \secref{study_overview}, we describe the overview of our study. In~\secref{study_setup}, we provide a detailed explanation of the datasets and LLMs selection process, as well as the preparation of prompts. In~\secref{study_design}, we introduce our experiments and analyze the obtained results. In~\secref{rel_work}, we review relevant literature. In~\secref{threat} We examine potential threats to the validity of our experiments. Finally, we present our conclusions in~\secref{conclusion}.

\section{Study Overview}
\label{sec:study_overview}
This section provides an overview of our study designed to address our research questions (~\figref{study_overview}). First, we conduct an empirical experiment to evaluate the ability of LLMs to identify different types of user feedback. For this purpose, we utilized manually labeled datasets (human-labeled datasets) from various publicly available sources studied in previous research, including app stores (4 datasets), X platform (2 datasets), and forums (2 datasets). Subsequently, we carefully prepared appropriate prompts to guide the models in a Zero-Shot Setting (ZSS), incorporating category definitions obtained from relevant studies. Finally, we performed a comprehensive analysis of the experimental results to answer our first research question.

Our analysis indicates that a suitable classification scheme can significantly enhance prediction accuracy. Accordingly, we designed a second experiment to evaluate the model's accuracy in predicting precise and coarse-grained categories. This improvement in accuracy is crucial as the consensus of LLMs can serve as high-quality data annotation method, addressing the critical challenge of limited labeled dataset. Transformer-based models (like BERT~\cite{Bert_model}, DistilBERT~\cite{Distilbert_model}, and RoBERTa~\cite{Roberta_model}) have demonstrated promising results in feedback classification in previous research. These models are lighter and more efficient for classifying large volumes of user feedback compared to LLMs. However, they consist of millions of parameters, and fine-tuning them requires a substantial amount of labeled data. Consequently, limited training dataset can significantly impact the performance of these models. On the other hand, the dynamic nature of user feedback and the continuous growth of applications in app stores necessitate ongoing updates to the training dataset, posing another significant challenge.

