Given the improved accuracy of LLMs in the coarse-grained classification scheme, which includes three main categories, bug report, feature request, and other, we investigated how LLMs can be leveraged to augment training datasets in our third experiment. Recent research~\cite{Abedini} has demonstrated that augmenting labeled datasets, especially with app-specific data, can enhance the performance of trained classifiers. In this experiment, we investigate the potential of LLMs in automatically annotating general-purpose and app-specific datasets. We prepare augmented datasets by integrating LLM-labeled and human-labeled datasets to fine-tune the feedback classification models more robustly. In the rest of this paper, we describe each experiment in detail.

\section{Study Setup}
\label{sec:study_setup}
\subsection{Selecting Datasets}
\label{sec:select-dataset}
User feedback provides valuable insights for developers at various stages of application evolution~\cite{Maalej_Journal,Chen2,Villarroel}. In recent years, researchers have analyzed user feedback from multiple perspectives and proposed various classification schemes tailored to different objectives~\cite{Hadi}. To ensure a comprehensive evaluation, we carefully selected human-labeled datasets based on the following criteria: (i) rigorous labeling processes, (ii) diverse sources with varying classification schemes, and (iii) adaptability to a coarse-grained classification scheme. Accordingly, we chose datasets extracted from three common user feedback sources.~\tabref{Adopt_Labels} provides detailed information about the datasets used in this study.

\textbf{App Store Datasets:}
App stores, as the most popular platforms, enable users to share their feedback on apps easily. They provide information on a wide range of apps, making them a valuable source for user feedback analysis. As a result, a substantial portion of past research has primarily focused on analyzing data extracted from app stores~\cite{Dabrowski}. In this study, we utilize four human-labeled datasets that have also been considered in previous empirical studies~\cite{Devin,Hadi}. Notably, a portion of dataset DS3 overlapped with dataset DS2. To avoid redundancy and bias in the results, we removed the overlapping instances from DS3~\cite{Abedini}. These datasets were collected from Google Play and the App Store platforms, containing user reviews from various apps. The number of apps represented in each dataset varies, ranging from 7 apps in DS1 to 458 apps in DS2. Additionally, an analysis of the classification schemes of these datasets reveals that they encompass various types of information within the reviews, classified at different levels of granularity. For instance, DS1 includes eight target classes for classifying user reviews, while DS3 employs a more abstract classification scheme with higher-level classes.

\textbf{Forum Datasets: }
Another source that can include user feedback is online forums related to apps, which can be useful for developers in identifying app requirements~\cite{Tizard, Iqbal}. These forums typically have an active community of users and developers who engage in diverse discussions in an interactive environment to assist and guide users. Tizard et al.~\cite{Tizard} demonstrate that 18 different types of information are provided in forums, which can offer valuable insights for developers. We use two datasets, DS5 and DS6, which were collected from the Reddit, VLC, and Firefox forums.

\textbf{X Datasets: }
Platform X, formerly known as Twitter, is another source for gathering user feedback about applications~\cite{Guzman_2}. Nayebi et al.~\cite{Nayebi} demonstrated that analyzing tweets can complement the information gained from app store reviews. Although tweets are shorter than app store reviews, they still provide crucial information for developers~\cite{Guzman_2}. Ignoring them may result in the loss of valuable information. Previous studies~\cite{Guzman_3,Guzman_2,Nayebi,Martens,Williams} introduced various approaches to analyzing information related to app evolution on this platform. In this study, we utilize two human-labeled tweet datasets, DS7 and DS8.

To ensure more accurate analyses, we removed samples from these datasets that did not provide sufficient information. Accordingly, instances that contained at least three words after common preprocessing steps, including lowercasing, removal of numbers, punctuation, stopwords, and non-informative characters (such as \$ and \#), were retained~\cite{Assi}. 

In this study, we also investigate the impact of classification granularity on the prediction accuracy of LLMs (RQ2). As can be seen, previous research has proposed various classification schemes across different sources based on the analysis of user feedback samples. For instance, Guzman et al.~\cite{Guzman} (in DS1) introduced an eight-class taxonomy for classifying user reviews in app stores, whereas Jha et al.~\cite{Jha} (in DS3) considered a higher-level classification scheme for user feedback with three classes. To create a more abstract classification scheme, we focused on two primary classes: ``\emph{bug report}'' and ``\emph{feature requests},'' which are crucial for requirements management. The remaining fine-grained classes were grouped under the ``\emph{other}'' category. We selected this coarse-grained classification as the focus of our study, as it is commonly of interest to developers and has been used in prior research~\cite{Devin,Hadi,Abedini}. 

