\subsection{Selecting LLMs}
In this study, our goal is to evaluate the capability of LLMs to facilitate the identification of different types of user feedback. Therefore, we selected the closed-source models GPT-3.5-turbo~\cite{GPT-3.5} and GPT-4o~\cite{GPT-4o}, along with the open-source models Llama3~\cite{Llama3} and Flan-T5~\cite{Flan-t5}. These models were chosen based on their applicability during our study and their established performance across various tasks in previous studies~\cite{Dvivedi,Ye}. They are autoregressive transformer-based models that have been pre-trained on extensive datasets\cite{GuBrown,Chung}. The first two models are recent releases from OpenAI's GPT-3.5 and GPT-4 series, known for their high accuracy and fast response times~\cite{GPT-3.5-Turbo-Updates,hello-gpt-4o}. GPT-3.5-turbo is optimized for chat and demonstrates better instruction understanding than earlier GPT-3 models while offering lower costs~\cite{Ye}. GPT-4o enhances the capabilities of its predecessors and provides higher speed~\cite{Introducing-GPT-4o}. It achieves performance comparable to GPT-4 Turbo in various text benchmarks~\cite{hello-gpt-4o}. As a result, they can be suitable candidates for conducting our designed experiments.

Llama3, introduced by Meta~\cite{meta-llama-3}, has demonstrated competitive results with GPT-4o across various tasks~\cite{Dubey}. Comparing its capabilities with GPT-series models in classifying user feedback can help developers select the most suitable model for their specific objectives. In this study, we utilize a pre-trained Llama3 model with 70 billion parameters~\cite{Llama3}. Moreover, the three previously considered models in this study follow a decoder-only architecture. To ensure a more comprehensive evaluation, we also include Flan-T5~\cite{Flan-t5}, an encoder-decoder model, as one of our target models. Flan-T5 enhances the performance of the T5 model through instruction fine-tuning~\cite{Chung}, making it a valuable addition to our analysis. 

\subsection{Preparing Prompts}
LLMs have been pre-trained on massive datasets~\cite{GPT-3.5,GPT-4o,Llama3,Flan-t5}, enabling them to excel across various downstream tasks without requiring extensive context-specific data~\cite{He}. In zero-shot learning, the quality of the prompt plays a crucial role in guiding the model to generate accurate and relevant responses. A well-structured prompt defines the task, provides necessary context, and instructs the model to produce outputs in a specified format~\cite{Colavito}. Given the impact of prompt design on model performance, we carefully construct a task-specific prompt based on prior research~\cite{Colavito}. This prompt effectively introduces the user feedback classification task and ensures the model receives the essential information for accurate predictions. 

Therefore, the prompt we provide to the model in our experiments consists of two main sections: prompt context and prompt instruction. The prompt context section includes essential background information to guide the model according to our objectives. This section provides a description of the target task, an introduction to the target categories, and the provision of several examples (if required by the experiment). For the original classification schemes, category definitions are extracted from the related research papers and introduced to the model. Consequently, the same categories may have different names and definitions across different datasets. For example, the category related to bug reports are present in all datasets, but each describes it differently. Also, this category is referred to by different names. In DS1, DS3, and DS6, it appears as ``bug report,'' in DS2 and DS7, as ``bug,'' in DS4 as ``functional bug report,'' in DS5 as ``apparent bug,'' and in DS8 as ``Problem Report.'' 
For the coarse-grained classification scheme, we constructed comprehensive definitions by integrating descriptions of these categories. These definitions effectively represent the corresponding samples by outlining the purpose of each category and highlighting the distinctions between them, which aids the model in precisely identifying the intents of user feedback.

The second section of the prompt includes our request to the model to classify the user feedback along with the user feedback sample to be classified. We instruct the model to assign the most appropriate categories for the feedback based on the context provided in the first section of the prompt. Accordingly, we design prompts tailored to the objectives of each experiment. The specific prompts used in each experiment are detailed in the artifacts link for reproducibility.

\section{Study Design and Implications}
\label{sec:study_design}
To address our research questions, we designed three experiments. First, we evaluate the capability of LLMs to classify user feedback in a zero-shot setting (RQ1). We use eight human-labeled feedback datasets (DS1–DS8) from diverse sources, each featuring different classification schemes and abstract levels. To predict the category of each feedback instance using LLMs, we formulated individual requests with appropriate prompts. As previously explained, each prompt includes a description of the predefined categories corresponding to each dataset, extracted from their original research papers. Due to the context length limitations of LLMs, we cannot provide examples for all categories across datasets, especially for datasets with more categories, such as DS1, which contains eight categories. As a result, we conduct this experiment in a zero-shot setting.

Since the accuracy of the results obtained in the first experiment was unsatisfactory for some categories of our target datasets, we designed the second experiment to evaluate whether using a well-defined, coarse-grained classification scheme with distinct categories could improve the performance of LLMs (RQ2). To this end, we focused on three primary categories: ``\emph{Bug Report}'', ``\emph{Feature Request}'', and ``\emph{Other}'', which have been widely studied in previous research~\cite{Devin,Hadi}. We mapped the categories of our datasets to this scheme (~\secref{select-dataset}). We then analyzed the capability of LLMs in predicting these categories under zero-shot and few-shot settings. Given the reduced number of categories, we were able to include examples (one per class) to provide better guidance to the models. These examples were randomly selected from the dataset samples and excluded from the overall test set to prevent data leakage. 

The results of our second experiment showed that while the accuracy of feedback classification by LLMs on the forum and X datasets remains unsatisfactory, notable  improvements were observed on app store datasets. 
Furthermore, our analysis indicates that prediction accuracy can be substantially enhanced through the consensus of LLM predictions. Samples assigned the same label by all four LLMs are highly likely to be reliable.
In the third experiment, we investigated whether leveraging LLMs as annotators could augment existing human-labeled datasets to train more efficient models, mitigating the limitations of LLMs and enhancing their practicality for real-world deployment (RQ3).
To investigate this, we selected a dataset of unlabeled user feedback, where the target category was first predicted by our four studied LLMs. The subset of samples for which the LLMs reached a consensus on the label was used as the augmentation dataset.

Previous research~\cite{Abedini} has shown that incorporating app-specific data can yield more accurate classifiers. To provide a more comprehensive analysis, we randomly selected user feedback for constructing the LLM-labeled dataset from general and app-specific datasets. 
The general dataset consists of over 2.5M feedback from 1,132 apps across 29 non-gaming categories. The app-specific datasets include over 100K recent user feedback from selected target apps in Google Play. We collect user feedback using the google-play-scraper library~\cite{google-play-scraper}.
Since DS1 explicitly identifies the app associated with each review, we use it as the truth dataset for evaluating fine-tuned models. This dataset includes reviews for seven apps. Due to resource and space limitations, we selected three apps as target apps for this experiment. Additionally, DS2, DS3, and DS4 contribute to the human-labeled portion of the training dataset.
We prepare the LLM-labeled datasets to be added to the existing human-labeled datasets. This augmentation allows us to examine the impact of increased training data volume on the classification model's performance. We maintain an equal ratio of 0.3 between LLM-labeled and human-labeled samples across different categories, as detailed in~\secref{rq3}. 

% Specifically, we added 1,563 LLM-labeled feedback samples to the \emph{``other''} category, 1,488 to the \emph{``bug report''} category, and 740 to the \emph{``feature request''} category.

To train the user feedback classification model, we use Transformer-based models, which have demonstrated state-of-the-art performance in feedback classification~\cite{Hadi}. We fine-tune binary classification models for the bug report and feature request categories based on the pre-trained DistilBERT model~\cite{Distilbert_model}, using the AutoModels function~\cite{TFAutoModel} from the Hugging Face library. Since some categories have a small volume in our datasets, we apply 5-fold cross-validation to ensure robust evaluation. Additionally, we address the category imbalance issue by incorporating category weights during model training using the class\_weight parameter.

To access the three LLMs, GPT-3.5, GPT-4o, and Llama3, we sent individual requests for each sample to them through the API of their respective service providers. We execute the Flan-T5 model on a free GPU provided by the Google Colab platform. While some model outputs explicitly indicated the predicted category, in many cases, additional processing was required to extract the relevant categories. To streamline this process, we developed a function that automatically extracts the predicted class from the model output. Additionally, for cases where multiple predicted categories were extracted, manual reviews were conducted to verify the accuracy of the final processed outputs. Further details on hyperparameter values, requested prompts, and additional processing can be found in our shared artifacts.

\subsection{RQ1: LLMs for Feedback Classification}
In this experiment, we assess the accuracy of user feedback classification based on the primary classification scheme using target LLMs.~\tabref{RQ1_results} presents the evaluation results of the LLM predictions. The maximum and minimum values for each metric in each row are highlighted in bold and underlined, respectively.
As observed, employing various LLMs in a zero-shot setting leads to an average increase in precision from 9.4 to 14.0, recall from 1.0 to 5.1, and F1 from 1.5 to 13.6 compared to the fine-tuned model. The notable improvement in F1 is primarily due to a significant increase in precision, while the recall shows only a modest improvement, with a decrease observed for the Flan-T5 model. Therefore, LLMs do not guarantee a higher probability of identification of all instances within a category. One possible reason is the variation in feedback labeling policies, which allows the model to be fine-tuned on the same dataset to identify various instances with probabilities comparable to stronger LLMs. Furthermore, since LLMs are general-purpose models and not specifically fine-tuned for user feedback classification, substantial improvements in identifying all types of feedback instances are not guaranteed.

The performance comparison of the four LLMs indicates that GPT-4o achieved the best results, with an average F1 of 60.3. Notably, GPT-4o achieved the highest F1 across all datasets except DS6. GPT-3.5 and Llama3 demonstrated competitive results, with average F1 of 57.7 and 57.1, respectively. The Flan-T5 model exhibited the lowest performance, with an average F1 of 48.2, consistently ranking lowest across all datasets except DS8. This may be attributed to the richer and more diverse training data used for GPT-4o, GPT-3.5, and Llama3, which enables better generalization in the classification tasks of this study.

