Across all datasets, the use of GPT-3.5, GPT-4o, and Llama3 for predicting user feedback categories resulted in increased precision and F1. While Flan-T5's predictions generally followed this trend for these metrics, decreases were observed in two datasets for precision and three datasets for F1, compared to the fine-tuned model. Conversely, the average recall results for the LLMs exhibited variation, particularly in app store user review datasets, where the average recall of LLM predictions decreased by 9.6 to 26.8 compared to the fine-tuned model. Therefore, while LLMs achieved higher precision for app store feedback, they did not consistently identify a greater proportion of instances within each target category. This also explains why the F1 improvements in app store datasets were less pronounced than those from other sources.

The results for the forum and X datasets indicate that, except in a few cases, using LLMs led to improvements across all three evaluation metrics. The maximum improvements in the average F1 achieved by LLMs were 21.5 for forums, 14 for X, and 11.5 for app stores. Consequently, a comparative analysis of average metrics across platforms reveals that the performance improvement relative to the fine-tuned model was more significant for the forum datasets than for X and app stores. This outcome is expected, as the forum datasets had fewer samples per class than the other datasets in the study. The limited sample size in forums contributed to the lower performance of the fine-tuned model, making the relative gains achieved by LLMs more pronounced.

Moreover, it is observed that the highest F1 are often associated with DS7 and DS3, while the lowest F1 correspond to DS5 and DS1. The stronger performance on DS7 and DS3 can be attributed to their coarse-grained categories and the presence of relatively comprehensive definitions for each category. Such clarity is especially valuable in a zero-shot setting, where well-defined categories help models better understand distinctions and enhance prediction accuracy. On the other hand, DS5 and DS1 feature more diverse classification schemes with closely related categories. This conceptual similarity makes it more challenging to distinguish between samples, leading to a higher rate of classification errors. For example, in DS1, 17.6\% and 26.6\% of samples labeled under the ``feature shortcoming'' category were misclassified as ``bug reports'' and ``complaints,'' respectively, conceptually closest categories. 

Additionally, 27\% of the samples classified under ``Feature Strength'' were misclassified as ``Praise.'' This can be attributed to the inherent overlap between these categories, as user satisfaction with a feature often results in both highlighting its strengths and expressing appreciation towards the developers As a result, the conceptual similarity between these two categories makes it challenging for models to differentiate them accurately. This challenge is further observed in the DS5 dataset, where several categories also exhibit closely related concepts. For example, 26.0\% of samples in the ``Application Guidance'' category and 11.7\% in the ``Feature Request'' category were misclassified as ``Usage.'' Such confusion highlights how ambiguous or overlapping definitions can lead to errors, especially when models have not been explicitly trained on datasets structured around these nuanced distinctions. This underlines the importance of clearly defined coarse-grained categories for improving classification accuracy, especially in zero-shot settings.


