\subsection{RQ2: LLMs for Coarse-Grained Feedback Classification}

Based on the results of the previous experiment, we found that datasets with a fine-grained classification scheme exhibited lower predictability by LLMs. Therefore, in this experiment, we aim to investigate whether adopting a coarse-grained classification scheme, where categories are distinct and well-defined, enhances model accuracy. To achieve this, we adapted our human-labeled datasets to the desired coarse-grained categories (DS\{1-8\}-Adopted).~\tabref{RQ2_results_zero} and ~\tabref{RQ2_results_few} present the results for feedback classification in zero-shot and few-shot settings, respectively. The maximum and minimum values for each metric in each row are highlighted in bold and underlined. During the dataset adaptation process, samples belonging exclusively to categories that could not be aligned with the defined proposed scheme were excluded. Therefore, for a more precise analysis, we reported the average of evaluation metrics for prediction of the target categories based on the original classification scheme in the zero-shot setting (DS\{1-4\}-Targeted). For other datasets where no changes were made to the samples, the value of evaluation metrics based on the original classification scheme remain consistent with those in~\tabref{RQ1_results}.

As observed, using a coarse-grained classification scheme improved feedback category prediction in the zero-shot setting in most cases. The predictions of the studied LLMs under this new classification scheme showed an average increase of 10.1 in F1, 9.8 in recall, and 5.1 in precision for the app store dataset, representing the most significant improvement across all platforms. Similarly, forum datasets showed an average increase of 4.6 in F1 4.6, 6.2 in recall, and 5.1 in precision. Across all datasets from these platforms, F1 and precision values improved. Conversely, the results from platform X indicated a slight average decrease of 1.8 in F1, 3.9 in recall, and 0.9 in precision. This decrease could be due to the introduction of new feedback category definitions, as the original definitions were dataset-specific. Furthermore, it might be influenced by uncertainty in LLMs. 

