On the other hand, the datasets DS5, DS4, and DS1, which contained more categories, showed the most improvement compared to predictions based on the original classification, with average F1 increases of 27.3, 9.2, and 7.9, respectively. This result was expected, as the new classification scheme provided more comprehensive definitions than their predefined categories and increased the level of abstraction. For example, in the dataset DS1, the average recall under the original classification was 57.0, 10.8 lower than the fine-tuned model. This may be due to the minimal distinctions between the ``bug report'', ``feature shortcoming'', and ``complaint'' categories, which could introduce errors in correctly identifying user feedback categories.
Additionally, in the dataset DS4, ``performance'' and ``bug report'' samples exhibited significant similarities, leading to 22.8\% of performance samples misclassified as bug reports. This indicates that LLMs struggle with predicting fine-grained categories accurately. Defining coarse-grained categories can help improve the classification of user feedback. Moreover, the dataset DS2, which underwent minor changes in its classification scheme, and the DS3 dataset, where the predefined categories remained unchanged, showed improvements in F1 and recall. These improvements were due to using more comprehensive definitions in the request prompts, which enhanced the clarity of category descriptions.

In this experiment, we also examine the performance of LLMs in the few-shot setting. Given the context limitations, we randomly select one sample per category from the same dataset.~\tabref{RQ2_results_few} presents the obtained results. As observed, in most cases, the performance of LLMs in the few-shot setting shows a slight decrease compared to the zero-shot setting, with the average F1 dropping by 1.1 in the app store datasets, 1.4 in the forum datasets, and 1.2 in the X datasets. This result could be due to the limited number of examples, which cannot effectively guide the model across the various types of samples within each category. Therefore, providing a small number of examples for LLMs may not lead to improved results.

The comparison of predictions from the studied LLMs reveals no significant difference between them, with a maximum difference of 2.4 in the overall average F1 in the zero-shot setting and 1.5 in the few-shot setting. In most cases, the maximum values of the evaluation metrics for LLMs in both zero-shot and few-shot settings are associated with the GPT-3.5 and GPT-4o models, indicating that these models have superior prediction capabilities. Additionally, the minimum recall values are often associated with the Flan-T5 model. Given that the training data for this model is smaller than that of the other models, it was expected that it would have less comprehensiveness and achieve lower success in predicting all types of feedback.

