On the other hand, another significant challenge in classifying user feedback is the lack of labeled data. One advantage of improving the prediction accuracy of LLMs is their potential to act as annotators for labeling up-to-date data, thereby enhancing manually labeled datasets. Based on the results obtained, while using LLMs with a coarse-grained scheme can improve the accuracy of user feedback classification, they did not achieve the necessary precision to generate new labeled datasets. The precision metric, which reflects the percentage of correct predictions for user feedback categories, varied between 47.6 and 88.9 across the datasets in the zero-shot setting. Therefore, the samples generated by the LLMs lack sufficient accuracy to create a labeled dataset that would effectively aid in training a feedback classification model.

Since different LLMs were trained on varied datasets, their consensus in labeling samples can lead to reliable predictions.~\tabref{RQ2_results_unified} presents results for samples assigned the same label across our different LLMs. The best results correspond to the app store datasets, except for DS2, with an average F1 of 91.8, precision of 93.8, and recall of 90.3. The discrepancy in DS2’s results compared to other app store datasets can be attributed to subtle variations in its labeling policy. Generally, higher precision indicates more reliable predictions, while higher recall suggests better identification of diverse samples. Given the relatively high Precision and Recall in app store datasets, we conclude that LLM-generated unified labels can be valuable for fine-tuning lighter models. In the following research question, we will further explore this hypothesis.



