\subsection{RQ3: LLMs for Data Augmentation}
\label{sec:rq3}
In this experiment, our goal is to utilize LLMs as annotators to augment existing human-labeled datasets and improve the accuracy of the feedback classification model. In RQ2, we demonstrated that datasets labeled through the consensus of LLM predictions can achieve high accuracy. Therefore, we apply this approach to generate an LLM-labeled dataset of user feedback, which is then used to augment the training dataset for fine-tuning a more robust model.

In previous experiments, we demonstrated that leveraging LLM predictions can enhance the performance of feedback classification models fine-tuned on human-labeled datasets. Additionally, a recent study by Devin et al.~\cite{Devin} showed that combining human-labeled datasets can improve feedback classification performance. Therefore, we established three 
baselines for this experiment:  
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Fine-Tuned Model}: A feedback classification model fine-tuned on the human-labeled dataset. 
    \item \textbf{Zero-Shot Model}: Target LLMs are applied in a zero-shot setting for feedback classification tasks. 
    \item \textbf{Review-Aug Model}: A feedback classification model fine-tuned on a human-labeled dataset augmented with another human-labeled dataset. 
\end{enumerate}

Since the DS2 dataset contains a limited number of samples, it cannot be used as an augmentation dataset. Therefore, we define two experimental settings:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{DS2-DS3 Setting}: DS2 and DS3 serve as the primary human-labeled dataset, and DS4 is used as the augmentation dataset in the Review-Aug model.
    \item \textbf{DS2-DS4 Setting}: DS2 and DS4 serve as the primary human-labeled dataset, and DS3 is used as the augmentation dataset in the Review-Aug model. 
\end{enumerate}

Next, we fine-tune feedback classification models using primary human-labeled datasets augmented with LLM-labeled datasets. The augmentation dataset is selected using one of two approaches: (i) Randomly selected augmentation, resulting in the Random-Aug model, and (ii) App-specific augmentation, resulting in the App-Specific-Aug model.
