\subsection{User profiling}
\label{sec:State of the Art:User profiling}
\noindent

User profiling through network traffic analysis involves constructing behavioral patterns of users based on their digital activities. This subsection reviews various methodologies used to extract user profiles from network traffic parameters. Each approach leverages distinct aspects of network communications to reveal behavioral patterns and user interests. We categorize the literature into three main groups: profiling based on HTTP/HTTPS parameters, DNS parameters, and broader network metadata, as summarized in Table~\ref{tab:user_profiling_summary}.

% Our focus is not on identifying specific individuals but on demonstrating how network traffic, particularly DNS queries, can reveal user behavior patterns. While previous studies have explored inferring detailed personal information from these profiles, our work aims to establish the fundamental vulnerability that exists when DNS traffic can be monitored. We demonstrate that this vulnerability requires protection mechanisms regardless of how the profile might be exploited later.

