Based on the power-law distribution of the inter-category and intra-category app usage we obtain a set of apps for each user. In our methodology, we assume that the probability of a user selecting a particular app is proportional to its installation probability. 
\paragraph{Category and weight assignation} First, we assign to each user the categories and its weight. In this case, the weights are equivalent to usage probabilities. 
We obtain samples from the power-law distribution of the categories. Each user gets a sample of this distribution. Because of the nature of the power-law distribution sampling, each user ends up with a unique weights vector, some categories may receive a zero weight while others receive a non-zero value. For example, user A obtains this categories and weights [`social: 68'; `communications: 11'; `business: 6'; `music and audio': 7; `games: 4';`entertainment: 4'; `shopping: 0'; `books and references: 0'; `news and magazines: 0'; `education: 0'].
