    \end{bmatrix}
\] 

Thus, we can calculate the shifting amount of sequences for different cell positions which means value of $k$. Amount of shift: $k=2$ for shift of cell position 0 with respect to 2, $k=3$ for shift of cell position 1 with respect to 0 and $k=5$ for shift of cell positioncell 0 with respect to 3. As this CA is a $4$-cell maximal length CA having a cycle length of 15; so phase shift with respect to $0^{th}$ cell are 3, 13 and 10 for $1^{st}$, $2^{nd}$ and $3^{rd}$ cell respectively. Based on the above characterization, we outline a method of finding the phase shift in a given $n$-cell maximal length CA (see Algorithm \ref{algorithm:phaseshiftoperation}) with respect to a {\bf predefined  cell position (pivot cell)}. For details of this algorithm and example, see~\cite{Nandi93d}.  

