\begin{prp}
	There is a 1-1 correspondence between r-VASSes
	and $\cV_r$-presheaves $X$ of finite type without
	``double'' edges,
	that is, if $e_1,e_2\in X[(\sfu,\mathbf{0})]$
	then $X[(\sigma_\sfu,\iota_{\mathsf{0}}^{\mathsf{0}})](e_1)
	\neq X[(\sigma_\sfu,\iota_{\mathsf{0}}^{\mathsf{0}})](e_2)$
	or
	$X[(\tau_\sfu,\iota_{\mathsf{0}}^{\mathsf{0}})](e_1)
	\neq X[(\tau_\sfu,\iota_{\mathsf{0}}^{\mathsf{0}})](e_2)$.
\end{prp}
\begin{proof}
  Checking that the operations presented above are inverses is
  straightforward.  Since $\cV_r$ and $\cG(\Z^r)$ are equivalent
  categories, the presheaf $X$ has finite type if and only if its
  restriction to $\cG(\Z^r)\times\{\mathsf{0}\}$ does.  The latter
  condition holds if and only if $Q_X$ and $E_X$ are both finite.
\end{proof}


\section{Automata with counters}
\label{s:Memory}

In this section we present an alternative and more general
construction than that of the previous section.  It prepares the
definition of higher-dimensional automata with counters in the
following section.


We regard a monoid $M$ as a d-category with one single object $*$ and
$M$ as a set of endomorphisms of $*$.  The only formorphism, and at the
same time backmorphism, is the identity.
\begin{df}
	Let $\cC$ be a d-category.
	A \emph{$\cC$-automaton with counter $M$} 
	is a $(\cC\times M)$-automaton.
\end{df}
$(\cC\times M)$-presheaves are naturally equivalent to functors
$\cC^{op}\to M\mhyphen\Set$ into the category of sets with an action
of $M$ and $M$-equivariant maps.

We show how VASSes can be realised as digraphs with counter $\N^r$,
extending the ideas of \cite{Meseguer-Montanari_PetriNetsAreMonoids}.
In the following examples, $\cG=\cG(\{a\})$ is the d-category from Section \ref{s:StdAut}
for a single letter alphabet.
\begin{exa}
	For a VASS $(Q,E)$ we define a $(\cG\times \N^r)$-automaton $X=X_{(Q,E)}$
	with
	\begin{itemize}
	\item
		vertices $X[\emptyset]=Q\times \N^r$, which correspond to configurations on $Q$,
	\item
		edges $X[a]=E\times \N^r$,
	\item
		$X[(\id_\emptyset,\sfw)](q,\sfv)=(q,\sfv+\sfw)$,
	\item
		$X[(\id_a,\sfw)](e,\sfv)=(e,\sfv+\sfw)$,
	\item
		$X[(\sigma_a,\sfw)](e,\sfv)=(\src(e), \sfv+\vvec(e)^-+\sfw)$,
	\item
		$X[(\tau_a,\sfw)](e,\sfv)=(\tgt(e), \sfv+\vvec(e)^++\sfw)$.
	\end{itemize}
\end{exa}

Yet not all $(\cG\times \N^r)$-automata may be realised this way.

\begin{exa}
	Let $m,n>0$ and let $X$ be a $(\cG\times \N)$-automaton with
	\[
		X[\emptyset]=\{x\}\times \{0,\dotsc,n-1\}\cup \{y\}\times \{0,\dotsc,m\},
		\qquad
		X[a]=\{v\}\times \N,
	\]
	\[
		X[\sigma_a](v,k)=(x, k \mod n),
		\qquad
		X[\tau_a](v,k)
		=
		\begin{cases}	
			k & \text{for $k<m$}\\
			m & \text{for $k \geq m$}.
		\end{cases}
	\]
	This can be obtained from an automaton corresponding to a VASS
        with two vertices $x,y$ and a single edge $e$ by identifying
        configurations in $x$ differing by $n$ and all configurations
        in $y$ with value at least $m$.  Yet this automaton does not
        correspond to any VASS since the set $X[\emptyset]$ is finite.
\end{exa}

\section{Higher-dimensional automata with counters}
\label{s:HDAC}

We conclude with introducing higher-dimensional automata with
counters, which generalise both higher-dimensional automata and
VASSes.

Fix an alphabet $\Sigma$.  Let
$\square(\Sigma)\subseteq \CList(\Sigma)$ be the skeleton of the
d-category from Section \ref{s:HDA}.  Recall that
$\square(\Sigma)$-presheaves and $\square(\Sigma)$-automata are
respectively precubical sets and higher-dimensional automata of
\cite{FJSZ_HDALang,FJSZ_Kleene-Arxiv}.

\begin{df}
	Let $\Sigma$ be an alphabet.
	A \emph{higher dimensional automaton with $r$ counters} (\emph{HDAC})
	is a presheaf automaton over $\sq(\Sigma)\times \N^r$.
      \end{df}

      van Glabbeek has shown that (infinite) HDA are more expressive
      than Petri nets \cite{vanGlabbeek_Expressiveness}.  His
      construction produces an HDA whose accessible part is finite
      only if the Petri net is safe.  Here we show that finite-type
      HDACs subsume all Petri nets -- with the safeness assumption
      lifted.

      Let $\cP=(P,T,F)$ be a labelled Petri net with places $P$,
      transitions $T$, flows $F\subseteq (P\times T)\sqcup(T\times P)$
      and a labeling function $\lambda:T\to\Sigma$.  Let the functions
      $\sfa,\sfb:T\to\{0,1\}^P$ be given by
\[
	\sfa(t)_p=
	\begin{cases}
		1 & \text{iff $(p,t)\in F$}\\
		0 & \text{iff $(p,t)\not\in F$}
	\end{cases}
	\qquad\text{ and }\qquad
	\sfb(t)_p=
	\begin{cases}
		1 & \text{iff $(t,p)\in F$}\\
		0 & \text{iff $(t,p)\not\in F$}
	\end{cases}.
\]
The associated HDAC $X_\cP\in\aut{\sq(\Sigma)\times \N^P}$ is defined by 
\[
	\Ob(\Cell X)
	=
	\{
		(t_1,\dotsc,t_n;\sfv)
		\mid
		t_i\in T,\; \sfv\in\N^P
	\},
\]
\[
	\pi_x(t_1,\dotsc,t_n;\sfv)
	=
	(\lambda(t_1)\intord \dotsm\intord \lambda(t_n)),
\]
\[
	X[(d^0_i,\sfw)](t_1,\dotsc,t_n;\sfv)
	=
	(t_1,\dotsc,t_{i-1},t_{i+1},\dotsc,t_n;\sfv+\sfa(t_i)+\sfw),
\]
\[
	X[(d^1_i,\sfw)](t_1,\dotsc,t_n;\sfv)
	=
	(t_1,\dotsc,t_{i-1},t_{i+1},\dotsc,t_n;\sfv+\sfb(t_i)+\sfw).
      \]
      This is essentially the construction in
      \cite{vanGlabbeek_Expressiveness}; the only difference being the
      enrichment of the underlying category.  In general, $X$ is not
      of finite type but a slight modifications of $X$ has this
      property.

Let $X_{nac}$ and $X_{{\leq}d}$ for $d\leq 1$ be the
sub-automata of $X$ given by
\begin{align*}
	X_{nac}[U]&=\{(t_1,\dotsc,t_n;\sfv) \in X[U] \mid \forall i\neq j.\; t_i\neq t_j \},
	\\
	X_{{\leq}d}[U]&=\{(t_1,\dotsc,t_n;\sfv) \in X[U] \mid n\leq d \}.
\end{align*}
In $X_{nac}$ we restrict to collections of different transitions
(that is, there is no autoconcurrency);
in $X_{{\leq}d}$ we restrict to collections of at most $d$ parallel transitions.

\def\sh{\mathsf{sh}} First we discuss a presentation of $X$ which is
not finite.  Let $\cE$ be the category with objects
\[
	\Ob(\cE)=\bigsqcup_{n\geq 0} T^n \cup \bigsqcup_{n\geq 0} T^n\times \{1,\dotsc,n\}\times\{0,1\}
\]
and non-identity morphisms 
\[
	\varphi_{(\bt,i,\varepsilon)}:(\bt;i,\varepsilon)\to \partial_i\bt,
	\qquad
	\psi_{(\bt,i,\varepsilon)}: (\bt;i,\varepsilon)\to \bt,
\]
for $n\geq 0$, $\bt=(t_1,\dots,t_n)\in T^n$, $1\leq i\leq n$, $\varepsilon\in\{0,1\}$
and
\[
	\partial_i\bt=(t_1,\dotsc,t_{i-1},t_{i+1},\dotsc,t_n).
\]
(Composition is determined by this data.)  
Let
$G:\cE\to \square(\Sigma)\times \N^P$ be a functor defined by
 \[
 	G(\bt)=(\bt;*),
 \]
 \[
 	G(\bt;i,\varepsilon)=(\partial_i\bt;*),
 \]
 \begin{equation} 
 \label{e:GPhi}
 	G(\varphi_{(\bt;i,\varepsilon)})=
 	\begin{cases}
 		(\id_{\partial_i\bt}, \sfa(t_i))  &\text{for $\varepsilon=0$}\\
 		(\id_{\partial_i\bt}, \sfb(t_i)) &\text{for $\varepsilon=1$}
 	\end{cases},
 \end{equation}
 \begin{equation} 
 \label{e:GPsi}
 	G(\psi_{(\bt;i,\varepsilon)}=(d^\varepsilon_i, \sfO).
\end{equation}
Finally, we define a map $f:\widehat{G}\to X_{\cP}$ determined by the functor $F:\cE\to\Cell X_\cP$:
\begin{equation}
\label{e:XPPresMap}
	F(\bt)=(\lambda\bt,\sfO)
,\qquad
	F(\bt;i,\varepsilon)=
 	\begin{cases}
 		(\lambda\partial_i\bt,\sfa(t_i)) & \text{for $\varepsilon=0$}\\
 		(\lambda\partial_i\bt,\sfb(t_i)) & \text{for $\varepsilon=1$}
 	\end{cases},
\end{equation}
where 
$\lambda\bt=(\lambda(t_1),\dotsc,\lambda(t_n))$.


\begin{lem}
\label{l:XPPres}
	The map $f$ is an isomorphism of presheaves.
\end{lem}
\begin{proof}
  It suffices to show bijectivity on elements.  Let $\tilde{f}$ be the
  composition
	\[
		C:=\coprod_{\bt} \widehat{G(\bt)}\to \widehat{G} \xrightarrow{f} X_{\cP}.
	\]
	Then $\tilde{f}$ is surjective since
        $(\bt;\sfv)=\tilde{f}(\id_\bt,\sfv)$.  Let $\sim$ be the
        equivalence on $\Ob(\Cell C)$ given by $x\sim y$ if and only
        if $\tilde{f}(x)=\tilde{f}(y)$.  Using (\ref{e:GPhi}) and
        (\ref{e:GPsi}) we obtain that every
        equivalence class of $\sim$ contains at
        most one element of the form $\bt$ for
        $\bt\in \coprod_{n\geq 0}T^n$. This implies injectivity.
\end{proof}

\begin{prp}
	The HDACs $X_{nac}$ and $X_{{\leq}r}$, for $r\geq 1$, have finite type.
\end{prp}
\begin{proof}
  Let $\cE_{nac}$ and $\cE_{\leq r}$ be the full subcategories of
  $\cE$ with objects $\bt=(t_1,\dotsc,t_n)$ and
  $(\bt;i,\varepsilon)$, such that either the
  $\lambda(t_i)$'s are pairwise different (for $X_{nac}$) or $n\geq r$
  (for $X_{{\leq}r}$). The restriction of $F$ (\ref{e:XPPresMap}) to
  $\cE_{nac}$ or $\cE_{\leq r}$ gives finite presentations of
  $X_{nac}$ and $X_{{\leq}r}$, respectively, by an argument similar to
  the proof of Lemma \ref{l:XPPres}.
\end{proof}


\section{Conclusion}

We have introduced presheaf automata as a generalisation of different
variants of higher-dimensional automata, introducing in particular
d-categories as a generalisation of the index categories that generate
them. We have shown that standard automata can be modelled as presheaf
automata as well, and likewise, as a more substantial contribution, Petri nets
without a safety assumption. We have also outlined the foundations of
a language theory for presheaf automata, including definitions of
regular and rational languages and a notion of open map for presheaf
automata, which forms the basis for notions of bisimulations. These
concepts may lead to analoga of classical theorems for automata, such
as Kleene's theorem, the Myhill-Nerode theorem or the
B\"uchi-Elgot-Trakhtenbrot theorem for some instances of presheaf
automata, extending recent work on higher-dimensional
automata~\cite{ABFM_Logic,FJSZ_Kleene-Arxiv,FZ_MyhillNerode-Conf}.


There is a long history of categorical approaches to standard automata
and a vast literature on the topic, starting, to our knowledge, with
work by Eilenberg and Wright
\cite{Eilenberg_Wright-AutomtaInGeneralAlgebras}, and both from an
algebraic and a coalgebraic point of view, as the following
representative texts show~\cite{Adamek-Trnkova_Automata,Rutten00}. A
recent functorial approach to standard automata has been described by
Colcombet and Petrisan~\cite{ColcombetP19}, who
cite~\cite{Bainbridge74} as an early predecessor. A presheaf approach
to standard automata has been proposed by
Rosenthal~\cite{Rosenthal95}, but using relational presheaves, which
are lax functors $\cC^{\textup{op}}\to \mathbf{Rel}$ into the category
of sets and binary relations. Both approaches have little relationship
to ours.

There is also a long line of work on transition systems and Petri nets
as presheaves, starting
with~\cite{Joyal-Winskel-Nielsen_Bisimulation}. The seminal article of
Meseguer and Monanari \cite{Meseguer-Montanari_PetriNetsAreMonoids} is
similar in spirit to our examples in later sections, but it emphasises
symmetric monoidal categories, which are not explicit in our work,
while presheaves are missing. Formalising cubical sets as presheaves,
as well as their simplicial or globular counterparts, is standard in
mathematics. van Glabbeek \cite{vanGlabbeek_Expressiveness} and
subsequently Goubault and Mimram \cite{Goubault-Mimram_Relationship}
have previously related higher-dimensional automata with other models
of concurrency, including transition systems, event structures or
Petri nets. By contrast, all comparisons from the previous sections
are original, to our knowledge,

Beyond the language-theoretic perspective mentioned above, we envisage
two main avenues for future work. Obviously, d-functors induce natural
transformations between presheaves over the source and target
d-categories. This provides tools for translating between different
instances of presheaf automata. A natural question asks for conditions
on d-functors that preserve various properties of presheaf automata.
Further, the relation between presheaf automata and monoidal
structures in concurrency, as for instance in
\cite{Meseguer-Montanari_PetriNetsAreMonoids}, remains to be explored,
see also the work of Grandis and Mauri for a monoidal approach to
cubical sets~\cite{Grandis-Mauri_Cubical}, the work of Sassone and
Soboci{\'n}ski and subsequently Baez and Master on open Petri nets in
the setting of symmetric monoidal bicategories and double
categories~\cite{SassoneS05,BaezM20}, as well as more recent work on
Petri nets by Baez et al~\cite{BaezGMS21}; see finally
\cite{FJSZ_HDALang} for a definition of parallel composition of
higher-dimensional automata in terms of a tensor.

