\section{Introduction}
The ability to handle multiple dynamic tasks concurrently and over long-term time windows \cite{delangecl2022survey,parisi2019continual} is a coveted feature of autonomous, adaptive agents that operate in open-ended environments involving physical-, vision-, and language-based tasks. Dynamic environments demand resilient agents that continuously learn useful behaviors from local data, generalize across diverse tasks, and effectively share useful knowledge \citep{TaraleDistributedLearning}. Such requirements are central to the emerging vision of agentic AI systems, which emphasize self-directed learning, adaptation, asynchronous operation, and scalable autonomy \citep{Colas2020AutotelicSurvey, Forestier2017IntrinsicallyLearning}. Recent work on collaborative and collective AI further highlights the importance of decentralized learning, peer-to-peer sharing, and modular reuse for building scalable societies of AI agents \citep{soltoggio2024nature}.

In such scenarios, reinforcement learning (RL) signals that can be sourced from the environment allow for policy optimization without external supervision, making RL an effective tool used both in robotics and language models \citep{sutton2018reinforcement, Jaderberg2016ReinforcementTasks, wang2025reinforcementlearningenhancedllms, sun2023reinforcementlearningerallms}. Crucially, knowledge reuse has been a central assumption in multi-task (MTRL) and meta reinforcement learning (Meta-RL) \citep{hospedales2021meta}, in which learning of many tasks is facilitated by exploiting similarities across tasks. The successful deployment of pre-trained models in LLMs is another example of effective reuse of previously acquired knowledge across tasks \cite{han2021pre,qiu2020pre}. Reliability of RL algorithms when dealing with evolving streams of data is improved with lifelong RL approaches \citep{ben2022lifelong}.

Most existing MTRL, Meta-RL, and lifelong RL methods assume a centralized setup with shared policy networks and known task labels \citep{Wang2023DMMatching, sun2022paco, Hendawy2023Multi-TaskExperts, Amani2023ScalingSharing, pmlr-v161-zeng21a, D'Eramo2020Sharing}. These assumptions limit their applicability in fully decentralized or real-world agentic scenarios, where agents typically operate independently, and communication, when possible, is asynchronous and not coordinated. To address these realistic conditions, recent work in lifelong learning and multi-agent systems explored machine-learned knowledge sharing among many agents \citep{nath2023L2D2-C, rostami2017multi, Gao2021KnowSR:Learning, ge2023lightweight, Gerstgrasser2023SelectivelyLearning}. Similar objectives are pursued by recent approaches that share and compose LoRA modules in LLMs \citep{huang2024lorahubefficientcrosstaskgeneralization, roy2025multlfgtrainingfreemultiloracomposition}, demonstrating that this challenge is crucial to scale current AI approaches. A full coverage of the related works in the literature can be found in appendix~\ref{sec:related_work}.

While the idea of sharing and reusing knowledge in agentic machine learning systems is intuitive and appealing, a key algorithmic problem has not yet been well addressed: in a collective of AI agents solving different tasks, what specific knowledge should be shared, with whom, and when? \cite{DARPA:ShELL,soltoggio2024nature} Moreover, does a decentralized collective of agents have an advantage over isolated agents learning the same tasks?  

To address these unanswered questions, this study proposes a new algorithm, \emph{Modular Sharing and Composition in Collective Learning (MOSAIC)}, that describes what policies to exchange among peers, specifically among which agents, when to do so, and how such policies can be integrated. Extending and integrating recent advances in lifelong machine learning, the approach employs modular knowledge representations in the form of network masks and proposes a knowledge exchange mechanism based on cosine similarity computed with Wasserstein task embeddings. Performance measures and similarities are used by individual agents to determine specifically what knowledge to retrieve, from whom, and when. 

The experimental evidence and the ablation studies show that precise selection of specific knowledge is essential to exploit the advantages of sharing. This evidence indicates that not all knowledge is beneficial to all agents, and that modular composition and cosine similarity metrics are essential ingredients in a collective of collaborating agents. MOSAIC agents learn faster than isolated agents by selectively leveraging the available knowledge from peers working on similar tasks. In some cases, by combining knowledge retrieved from other agents working on similar tasks, MOSAIC agents are able to solve tasks that are too difficult for isolated learners. Further analysis reveals that the collective implicitly discovers an ideal curriculum in which agents working on simpler tasks subsequently help agents working on harder tasks. The MOSAIC learning dynamics indicate that a collective of collaborative MOSAIC agents can achieve superior performance than that of an equivalent number of isolated learners, making a strong case for targeted knowledge sharing in agentic systems.

\subsection{Contributions}
This work introduces MOSAIC, an agentic system, i.e., an autonomous decision-making and self-contained individual agent, that implements autonomous and self-directed knowledge exchange with other MOSAIC peers. MOSAIC is a novel integration of the following algorithmic steps: (i) modularized knowledge learning and representation; (ii) online Wasserstein task embeddings computed from reinforcement learning datasets; (iii) knowledge querying and retrieval with cosine similarity (between embeddings) and performance-based selection heuristics.

The performance evaluations reveal significant learning advantages for a collective of collaborating agents with respect to isolated learners, which, in some cases, results in successful policies that cannot be discovered in isolation. 

Lastly, the analysis of learning dynamics revealed that agents choose what to learn in ways that lead to the emergence of implicit curricula, task clusters, and task hierarchies. This property enables an open-ended training behavior in which agents autonomously determine their learning trajectories by reusing specific subsets from the available collective knowledge.


