\section{Methodology}
The following section describes the components of the MOSAIC algorithm that includes knowledge representations (Section \ref{sec:modular}), Wasserstein task embedding  (Section \ref{sec:wasserstein_sim}), knowledge selection and sharing (Section \ref{sec:selection}), and knowledge composition (Section \ref{sec:knowledge_comp}). Finally, Section \ref{sec:summary_code} illustrates the integration of all components with pseudo-code. A visual summary of the main algorithmic steps is provided in Figure \ref{fig:approach}. 

\subsection{Policy Representations}
\label{sec:modular}
The ability to encapsulate specific knowledge in isolated module representations \citep{pmlr-v87-alet18a} is a key component of MOSAIC. To this aim, a variety of parameter isolation mechanisms from the field of lifelong learning can be used \citet{oswald2020continual_hypernet, hu2022lora, rusu2016progressive, mallya2018packnet}. Among those, promising results in lifelong RL were shown by network masks \citep{ben2022lifelong, wortsman2020supermasks} that are therefore used in this study. Masks are naturally suited for composition through linear combinations, which is an essential mechanism in MOSAIC. The use of masking makes MOSAIC agnostic to the specific reinforcement learning algorithm used, requiring only that policies are parameterized by a neural network. This allows it to be layered on top of any standard RL method, such as PPO \citep{schulman2017ppo}, DQN \citep{mnih2013playingatarideepreinforcement}, or A3C \citep{mnih2016asynchronousmethodsdeepreinforcement}. 
