\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}
MOSAIC is evaluated on three sparse-reward reinforcement learning benchmarks: MiniHack MultiRoom \citep{Samvelyan2021MiniHackResearch}, MiniGrid \citep{gym_minigrid}, and an image sequence learning task from CT-graph \citep{soltoggio2019ctgraph}. Each benchmark presents a curriculum of tasks with underlying structural commonalities, but also significant differences that result in some tasks being unrelated to others. The objective is to assess whether MOSAIC can autonomously identify and exploit these relationships to outperform agents trained in isolation or agents that blindly exchange all knowledge. Performance graphs show averaged results and 95\% confidence interval for various aggregations of experiments with a minimum of 5 seeds \citep{colas2018many}. The optimization of Eq.\ \ref{eq:objective} employed PPO \citep{schulman2017ppo} with a fully-connected network for the image sequence learning and MiniGrid tasks, and a convolutional network for MiniHack. The model architecture and hyperparameters (per module) are reported in the Appendix~\ref{sec:implementation_details} (Figure~\ref{fig:models} and Tables~\ref{tbl:hyperparameters_ppo}, \ref{tbl:hyperparameters_communication}, and \ref{tbl:hyperparameters_embeddings}).

\subsection{Image Sequence Learning}
\label{sec:ct-graph}
This benchmark is an RL problem based on the Configurable Tree Graph (CT-Graph benchmark \citet{soltoggio2019ctgraph}), where a set of procedurally generated navigation tasks implement a variable length RL problem, and where each node in a tree-graph is an RL state encoded as image. We use this benchmark to define a curriculum of 28 tasks with navigation paths of increasing depths of the graph (2 to 8) from four independent sets of images. This setting creates four sets of unrelated tasks with seven related tasks in each set. The seven tasks in a set have an increasing depth of the navigation tree, generating a range of RL tasks from easy (depth 2) to very hard (depth 8), effectively creating a hierarchy of task's complexity or difficulty. The sparse reward and exponential branching of the tree-graph structure make this benchmark a challenging RL setting, with reward probabilities as low as \(\approx 7.74 \times 10^{-9}\) for the most difficult tasks with depth 8 \citep{soltoggio2019ctgraph}.

Results in Figure~\ref{fig:ctgraph_minihack_results}(Top) show that MOSAIC achieves significantly faster learning and broader task coverage, outperforming MOSAIC-NoComm by 2.7\(\times\), reaching a maximum total return of 26.0 across all 28 tasks. MOSAIC reaches 50\% performance (14) in 37 iterations (18,944 steps). MOSAIC-NoComm plateaus at a maximum of 9.6. On average, MOSAIC-NoComm fails on 18 tasks, whereas MOSAIC fails on only 2.

\subsection{MiniHack MultiRoom}
MiniHack defines a grid-based navigation challenge with sparse rewards and pixel-based observations. Agents must navigate rooms and obstacles to reach a goal location. MOSAIC is evaluated on 14 navigation tasks organized into two clusters of increasing difficulty: one with room sizes of \(4\times4\), and another with \(6\times6\). Each level in a cluster adds an additional room, connected by a closed (but unlocked) door. The agent receives a reward of +1 for completing the task and a penalty of -0.01 for colliding with walls. The configuration of each task is randomly changed every episode, ensuring that agents must generalize across varying observations, goal locations, and door locations rather than memorize solutions.

In this experiment, agents must reuse sub-behaviors such as navigating to doors and exploring unseen regions to maximize performance across all tasks. Results in Figure~\ref{fig:ctgraph_minihack_results}(Bottom) show that MOSAIC agents collectively reach 0 return by iteration 132 (270,336 steps), compared to iteration 176 (360,448 steps) for MOSAIC-NoComm, which is a 25\% reduction in the number of samples to reach zero reward. Over the full training run, MOSAIC achieves a maximum total return of 9.04 across the 14 tasks. MOSAIC-NoComm peaks at only 3.96.
\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1.0\textwidth]{figures/mctgraph/cumulative.pdf}
    \includegraphics[width=1.0\textwidth]{figures/minihack/cumulative.pdf}
    \caption{Results comparing communicating MOSAIC agents versus isolated agents on the same set of tasks. (Top) Image sequence learning benchmark: average performance for 140 runs (28 tasks and five seeds/task) with 95\% confidence intervals. (Bottom) MiniHack Multiroom benchmark: average performance for 70 runs (14 tasks and 5 seeds/task) with 95\% confidence intervals. Sharing agents learn significantly better policies across more tasks in both benchmarks, with relative gains of 170.8\% and 128.2\% compared to the isolated learning baseline in the image sequence and MiniHack benchmarks, respectively.}
    \label{fig:ctgraph_minihack_results}
\end{figure}

\subsection{MiniGrid Crossing}  
The MiniGrid benchmark presents a sparse-reward grid-world navigation problem with symbolic observations. MOSAIC is evaluated on 14 tasks that span seven SimpleCrossing variants and their seven LavaCrossing counterparts. Agents must navigate around obstacles or hazardous terrain and reach a goal location. Variations in these tasks arise from differences in the layout, placement of objects, and structure of the room. Agents must learn and reuse generalizable skills across different variations without compromising their policies.

This curriculum evaluates MOSAIC's selection and composition mechanisms in a non-trivial task curriculum. In this setting, identifying reusable policies may be non-trivial, and tasks may have subtle interferences that may not be captured by the cosine similarity method. The evaluation focuses on whether knowledge acquired in SimpleCrossing tasks effectively transfers to LavaCrossing variants, as well as to other SimpleCrossing tasks. Figure~\ref{fig:minigrid_results} reports the summated return curves for each method. Table~\ref{tab:baseline_results} summarizes both the final and intermediate performance metrics.

MOSAIC is compared against representative baselines. Multi-Task PPO (MTPPO) shares a backbone across all tasks without modularity. Multi-DQN (MDQN) \citep{D'Eramo2020Sharing} attaches task-specific Q-heads to a shared encoder, enabling early selective feature reuse. Mixture of Orthogonal Experts (MOORE) \citep{Hendawy2023Multi-TaskExperts} enforces expert diversity via Gram-Schmidt projection. PCGrad+MOE \citep{yu2020gradientsurgerymultitasklearning} mitigates gradient conflict using a fixed expert pool. These baselines span different approaches to parameter sharing, task specialization, and modularity. Results on the MiniGrid benchmark are shown in Figure~\ref{fig:minigrid_results}.
