MOSAIC outperforms all baselines in final return, achieving a maximum total return of 11.67. The performance gap ranges from 1.08× over MOSAIC-NoComm to 37.65× over MDQN. MOORE demonstrates stronger early-stage performance, reaching 25\% and 50\% of the total return in 13 (26,624 steps) and 24 (49,152 steps) iterations, respectively, approximately 76.4\% and 73.3\% faster than MOSAIC. PCGrad+MoE also displays faster improvement in earlier iterations, reaching 25\% in 24 iterations (49,152 steps) and 50\% in 54 iterations (110,592 steps). In MTRL systems, parameter sharing enables early gradient sharing from all tasks into a shared backbone network, which quickly improves shared features even from weak or random policies. In contrast, MOSAIC relies on modular mask composition that is driven by task embeddings. In earlier stages, each task-specific mask and embeddings reflect a random policy. Without initial reward signals, the linear combination of such masks is ineffective or even harmful. This results in a bottleneck until iteration 100, which the baselines avoid by implicitly averaging across tasks. Both MOORE and PCGrad+MoE fail to reach 75\% performance, whereas MOSAIC continues improving, ultimately surpassing all baselines in both stability and final total return. MTPPO performs poorly, with a peak total return of 4.64, equating to 30\% of the maximum theoretical performance. MDQN fails to learn any tasks in this particular benchmark, peaking at a total return of 0.31.

\subsection{How Similarity Helps Targeted Policy Transfers}
To provide a better insight on the utility of similarity in knowledge reuse, Figure~\ref{fig:heatmaps} visualizes two key metrics: the average cosine similarity between task embeddings, and the average convergence values of the \(\beta\) parameters, denoted \(\hat{\beta}_j^i\), which are computed as average of the $\beta$ used in the consolidation operations (Eq.~\ref{eq:consolidation}). Each \(\hat{\beta}_j^i\) can be interpreted as a learned relationship from task \(j\) to task \(i\), estimating how useful a policy trained in task \(j\) is for learning task \(i\).
\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.72\textwidth]{figures/others/MOSAIC_mctgraph_clustering.pdf}
    \caption{Visualization of pairwise cosine similarity and \(\hat{\beta}\) statistics in the image sequence learning benchmark. (A) The cosine similarity matrix and (B) the \(\hat{\beta}\) values are shown to visualize what tasks are similar and which policies are used for which tasks. (C) The cosine similarity values are clustered using WPGMA, and the results are illustrated in a dendrogram. (D) The cosine similarity matrix is replotted with tasks arranged according to the clustering. The four clusters of tasks emerge clearly. (E) The clustered \(\hat{\beta}\) values show that similar tasks also have the highest indicator of policy reuse among each other. Annotated heatmaps of the individual clusters can be found in Figure~\ref{fig:zoom-grid-heatmaps} in the Appendix.}
    \label{fig:heatmaps}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:heatmaps}(A) shows cosine similarities for tasks in a random order, which is a default situation for an arbitrary set of unknown tasks. These values are clustered using WPGMA in Figure~\ref{fig:heatmaps}(C), producing a dendrogram that now clearly highlights four main groups of similar tasks as well as possible hierarchical relationships. Reordering the matrix accordingly (Figure~\ref{fig:heatmaps}(D)) makes these clusters more visually salient. Figures~\ref{fig:heatmaps}(B) and (E) show the corresponding \(\hat{\beta}_j^i\) matrices, before and after clustering. Notably, the clustered \(\hat{\beta}\) have a similar pattern to the similarity values, indicating that similar tasks are also those that most heavily reuse each other's policies during learning. 

The tasks in the Image Sequence Learning benchmark were purposely devised to be organized into four distinct image sets, each containing seven tasks that vary only in trajectory depth, from shallow to deep. Within each group, cosine similarity scores tend to be higher between tasks that are close in depth and lower between those that are further apart. This pattern suggests that MOSAIC embeddings capture both the visual similarity induced by the image distribution and the structural similarity defined by specific optimal policies. While the similarity matrix is symmetrical by definition, the \(\hat{\beta}\) values exhibit a more selective asymmetric reuse pattern that reflects the source-to-target nature of policy reuse. In addition, the initial weightings of \(\beta\) are tuned to maximize performance. The analysis reveals that similarity and knowledge reuse are highly correlated.

Finally, we investigate how the reuse is affected by the progression of learning across multiple tasks and multiple agents. Figure \ref{fig:ctgraph_individual} details the components of the performance graph (averaged in Figure \ref{fig:ctgraph_minihack_results}(A)) by grouping tasks by the length (depth of the CT-graph) in the image sequence learning benchmark. The learning dynamics reveal an interesting emergent property of the system. The knowledge reuse is affected by the fact that easier tasks are learned early, and their policies enable agents solving progressively more complex tasks. This analysis also explains the 170.8\% higher performance of communicating agents versus isolated agents in Figure \ref{fig:ctgraph_minihack_results}(A), which derives from the crucial sharing processes that enable agents on difficult tasks to retreive, combine and refine policies from simpler but related tasks, and find solutions to the most complex problems.

