\subsection{Ablation Studies}  
Ablation studies were conducted on the image sequence learning problem (Section \ref{sec:ct-graph}) to assess the effect on performance that emerges from the combination of the key algorithmic steps: the knowledge selection criteria and the weighted policy initialization.  Figure~\ref{fig:main_ablation} compares three ablated variants of MOSAIC: one without cosine similarity-based selection (criterion 1), one without performance-based selection (criterion 2), and one without reward-guided weight initialization. MOSAIC-NoComm is included as a reference baseline.

The ablation without cosine similarity-based selection (criterion 1) reaches a total return of 20.1. MOSAIC achieves 26.0, outperforming it by a factor of 1.29. We speculate that this gap could widen further in settings in which the number of unrelated tasks grows, i.e., where selecting the most relevant knowledge becomes critical. 

The second ablation study without performance-based selection (criterion 2) performs comparably with MOSAIC at the end of the training (25.6), but it shows significantly slower learning. These dynamics make sense because, as all agents improve their performance on their respective tasks, selecting for high reward becomes less critical. These two ablation studies (removal of criterion 1 or 2) reach 50\% of the theoretical maximum (14.0) at iterations 76 (38,912 steps) and 71 (36,352 steps), respectively. MOSAIC reaches this threshold at iteration 37 (18,944 steps), reaching 50\% performance 1.9\(\times\)\textasciitilde2.0\(\times\) faster than baselines.

The third ablation study removes the reward-guided initialization and instead uses a fixed weighting of 0.5 for the local policy and 0.5 for external policies. This experiment performs comparably with MOSAIC, however, the average return shows periodic instability, characterized by sharp performance drops coinciding with communication events, resembling a sawtooth pattern. This behavior suggests that the reward-guided initialization is a useful approach to maintain stability in performance when integrating external knowledge.

Further ablation studies are shown in Section~\ref{sec:appendx_ablations} to test the impact of querying frequency on the performance of MOSAIC, and the impacts of the number of samples and size of the reference distribution on the Wasserstein task embeddings.
