\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
The results, while showing different performance advantages across different tasks and curricula, indicate that the principles introduced in MOSAIC are effective in diverse settings. MOSAIC showed significantly faster and more successful learning dynamics with respect to isolated agents working on the same sets of tasks. These observations  strongly suggest that knowledge composition enables effective reuse of previously learned policies across tasks and agents, supporting similar observations in a centralized single-agent lifelong learning setting \citep{ben2022lifelong}. In particular, masks from some tasks were observed to accelerate learning when selectively transferred to agents dealing with other tasks. We conclude that the selection and compositional mechanisms lead to the discovery of successful policies with a significantly better sample efficiency. We observed the advantages to be particularly significant in sparse-reward and hierarchical reinforcement learning environments (image sequence learning with varying depth), where agents learning difficult tasks benefit from acquiring policies from agents that are learning simpler tasks, supporting findings in \citet{Florensa2017AutomaticAgents, Nachum2018Data-EfficientLearning, Uchendu2022Jump-StartLearning}.

The ablation studies highlight the importance of the selection criteria--cosine similarity for relevance, reward-based selection for utility--as well as the reward-based weighting to optimize the initial policies. The results of the ablation studies are particularly significant because they suggest that knowledge sharing and integration may not be effective in a vanilla setup where all knowledge is shared \citep{isele2018selective}. It is conceivable that such criteria are crucial for scalability, for example, when implementing a system of a large number of interconnected agentic AI working on a large set of diverse tasks in a fully asynchronous setting \citep{Foerster2016LearningLearning}.

Interestingly, removing the reward-guided initialization of policies introduces periodic instability. Despite achieving a slightly higher final return (26.4 vs. 26.0), this variant suffers sharp drops after communication events, suggesting that newly acquired policies can interfere with learning when not weighted adaptively. It is also conceivable that this setting could become critical for the safe deployment of MOSAIC: new externally acquired policies, even if vetted by criteria 1 and 2 that should ensure relevance and performance, may nevertheless require ``careful'' introduction in the agent's own policy \citep{Amodei2016ConcreteSafety}.

MOSAIC currently relies on raw episodic reward as a selection and weighting signal, which may limit generalization across environments with unstandardized or sparse reward functions. Future variants could use reward-normalized scores or task-progress metrics to support more environment-agnostic policy reuse \citep{Jaques2018SocialLearning}. Additionally, MOSAIC assumes a fully connected communication topology, which may not scale well in systems with many agents or bandwidth constraints; exploring adaptive or sparse topologies could improve scalability to real-world applications \citep{Kaimakamidis2024CollaborativeCommunity, Marino2025DecentralizedAgreements, tang2024collaborative}. The current design also restricts mask composition to positive, additive combinations, limiting the ability to suppress interference from poorly aligned peer policies. Introducing signed or non-linear composition strategies could address this issue \citep{He2021TowardsLearning}. Finally, the use of a shared, frozen backbone across agents may constrain representational diversity and reduce adaptability in highly heterogeneous task settings. Allowing for backbone specialization or dynamic composition techniques may improve transfer and robustness in more diverse environments \citep{pmlr-v97-houlsby19a}.

While this work uses masking as the modular knowledge representation, similar mechanisms based on parameter isolation could be tested. For example, LoRA-like modules or adapters have shown significant promise in transformer architectures and effectively act as pre-trained modules that have been shown to work also with various fusion and combination approaches \citep{He2021TowardsLearning,wu2024mixture,han2021pre,qiu2020pre}. This consideration opens the possibility for compositional reuse in transformer-based models and large-scale language agents.

Communication and selection in MOSAIC were introduced as a proof-of-concept to assess the validity of the hypothesis that knowledge selection (what and when to transfer \citep{DARPA:ShELL,soltoggio2024nature}) is a key element in agentic, decentralized, and fully asynchronous systems. To achieve an even higher level of agency in MOSAIC agents, more aspects related to communication could be learned by a policy \citep{Foerster2016LearningLearning}. Agents learning and optimizing their communication could lead to more dynamic behaviors that are better suited to each agent's task(s) and resources. However, learnable and dynamics communication policies could lead to potential safety concerns, as agents that learn to collaborate could learn greedy, or uncooperative policies that may benefit the agent but not the collective as a whole. In the simplest instance, an agent that does not share knowledge exploits the collective knowledge without contributing to it. In a worst-case scenario, malicious agents could share harmful policies. Such an extension of MOSAIC could lead to complex learning and collaboration dynamics described in Game Theory \citep{myerson2013game}. In summary, we are aware that the MOSAIC algorithm raises safety considerations \citep{hammond2025multi,bengio2025international}: without appropriate filters or constraints, agents may transfer misaligned or flawed policies, leading to instability or harmful behavior in sensitive environments. MOSAIC-like systems are also vulnerable to attacks by malicious actors who exploit the peer-to-peer nature, poisoning models, or extracting policies or training data \citep{Vassilev2024AdversarialMitigations}.

The knowledge composition mechanism in MOSAIC derives from approaches in lifelong machine learning, where agents encounter a stream of continuously changing tasks. Therefore, the algorithm is particularly suitable to be extended to a lifelong learning setting. In such an extension, each policy is a composition of three sources: a task-specific mask for the current task, prior masks from previously learned tasks, and peer-acquired masks from other agents. Effectively, MOSAIC is ideally designed to reuse prior knowledge from previous tasks when learning a continuous stream of tasks \citep{serra2018hat}. For these considerations, we hypothesize that the reuse and integration of policies acquired from other agents within a set of own-policies could enable scalable and open-ended lifelong learning \citep{leibo2019autocurricula,stanley2019open} across diverse task streams.


