\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}
The section provides additional information on the related work that contributed to MOSAIC.
\paragraph{Modular Representations and Masking.}
Modular policies promote parameter reuse by isolating task-specific substructures. Binary masks define sparse sub-regions of shared networks, reducing interference and enabling structured composition \citep{mallya2018piggyback, mallya2018packnet, ramanujan2020s, serra2018hat, wortsman2020supermasks, koster2022signing_sm}. Some approaches compose a mask to support task transfer \citep{Ostapenko2021ContinualComposition, mendez2022neuralcomposition, GummadiSHELS:BOUNDARIES,ben2022lifelong}. Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architectures route inputs to sparse expert modules via learned gates \citep{shazeer2017outrageouslylargeneuralnetworks, fedus22switchtransformers}, with variants adapted to multi-task and lifelong settings \citep{gururangan-etal-2020-dont, mendez2021lifelong}. MOORE imposes orthogonality constraints across experts to promote representational diversity \citep{Hendawy2023Multi-TaskExperts}. Related work in modular meta-learning emphasizes structural reuse through task-level module composition \citep{pmlr-v87-alet18a}. LoRA adds task-specific low-rank adapters to fixed models for efficient specialization \citep{hu2022lora}. Neural Module Networks support compositional inference via dynamically assembled submodules \citep{andreas16neural}. MultiCriticAL isolates value functions per task to mitigate interference in actor-critic training \citep{MysoreMULTI-CRITICSTYLE}. Recent robotic lifelong learning systems also adopt modular structures with Bayesian non-parametric inference to preserve and combine task-specific knowledge \citep{meng2025preserving}.

\paragraph{Distributed Knowledge Reuse.}
Modular reuse has been extended to distributed and federated RL. FedWeIT \citep{yoon2021fedweit} and CONFEDMADE \citep{Paul2023MaskedLearners} use attention-weighted masks or modular encoders to isolate transferable components. Other methods support cross-agent sharing of task-specific masks or subnetworks \citep{Gao2021KnowSR:Learning, ge2023lightweight, nath2023L2D2-C, rostami2017multi, Gerstgrasser2023SelectivelyLearning}. Recently, PRISM \citep{PRISM:MODELS} introduced a federated generative framework that communicates stochastic binary masks instead of full parameters. FedProto \citep{Tan2021FedProto:Clients} facilitates communication-efficient reuse via prototype aggregation. DiPaCo \citep{Douillard2024DiPaCo:Composition} and OpenDILoCo \citep{JaghouarOpenDiLoCo:Training} offer decentralized, low-communication frameworks for collaborative model training. Recent perspectives such as \citet{soltoggio2024nature} articulate a broader vision of decentralized collective AI, emphasizing modular architectures that enable asynchronous, interpretable knowledge exchange at scale. \citet{Diskin2021DistributedCollaborations}, \citet{mcmahan2017communication}, and \citet{meng2025preserving} also explore communication-efficient knowledge preservation and reuse in lifelong learning agents.

Peer-to-peer frameworks exchange representations via role-switching, consensus updates, or parameter interpolation \citep{SenguptaADISTILLATION, Alvar2019Multi-taskIntelligence, Kaimakamidis2024CollaborativeCommunity, javier2022collaborative, nadiger2019personalizedFL, Lozano-Cuadra2024ContinualRouting}. PaCo and similar methods interpolate within shared subspaces to define composable policies \citep{sun2022paco}. Model-based frameworks support decentralized planning with shared structured representations \citep{Jiang2021ModelEnvironment, Du2025ContextualCoordination, Shi2022KnowledgeTasks}. PEEPLL introduces confidence-based peer communication for decentralized lifelong learning, reducing reliance on environmental supervision by leveraging self-evaluated knowledge sharing between agents \citep{TaraleDistributedLearning}. CenRA \citep{Ma2024KnowledgeLearning} centralizes reward broadcasting, while jump-starting methods guide early-stage learning via reuse \citep{Uchendu2022Jump-StartLearning}.

\paragraph{Task Similarity and Inference.}
Task inference enables modular adaptation without task labels. Latent and Bayesian models estimate embeddings from trajectories \citep{Zintgraf2019VariBAD:Meta-Learning, Achille_2019_ICCV}, while non-parametric and prototype-based methods define task similarity via behavioral statistics \citep{yarats2021reinforcementlearningprototypicalrepresentations, Chu2023Meta-ReinforcementClustering, liu2022wasserstein, Kolouri2020WassersteinLearning, dick2024statistical}. Wasserstein barycenter methods enable structure-aware ensembling by incorporating semantic side information through optimal transport distances \citep{Dognin2019WassersteinEnsembling}, and learning embeddings into entropic Wasserstein spaces offers a flexible alternative to Euclidean embeddings for capturing rich relational structures \citep{Frogner2019LearningSpaces}. Contrastive objectives such as CURL \citep{Srinivas2020CURL:Learning} and ATC \citep{Stooke2020DecouplingLearning} improve representation learning, though they typically require encoder training and large batch sizes. Structure-aware distillation has also been explored in incremental graph-based settings \citep{Wang2021GraphSystems}, offering another avenue for modular task inference.

Gradient-based meta-RL methods such as MAML \citep{finn2017model} optimize for rapid task adaptation through fine-tuning, while variational approaches like VariBAD \citep{Zintgraf2019VariBAD:Meta-Learning} condition policy learning on inferred latent variables. In contrast, non-parametric methods that reuse task-conditional modules based on similarity measures offer an alternative path to fast generalization without the need for gradient-based adaptation.

\paragraph{Model Merging and Upcycling.}
Emerging techniques enable the combination and repurposing of existing models through weight-space merging or routing. \citet{Wortsman2022ModelTime} and \citet{Rame2023RewardedRewards} propose interpolating parameters from multiple fine-tuned models to improve generalization. TIES-Merging \citep{Yadav2023TIES-Merging:Models} resolves interference between merged models. \citet{Komatsuzaki2022SparseCheckpoints} and \citet{Sukhbaatar2024Branch-Train-MiX:LLM} demonstrate sparse upcycling: extracting sparse modular experts from dense checkpoints. These strategies provide alternatives to training from scratch and are critical for decentralized reuse.

\paragraph{Adaptive and Elastic Architectures.}
Recent work explores modular and resource-aware architectures. MatFormer \citep{Devvrit2023MatFormer:Inference} and Mixture-of-Depths \citep{Raposo2024Mixture-of-Depths:Models} dynamically allocate compute and adjust routing depths in transformer models. These approaches support efficient inference and specialization while preserving modular structure, principles aligned with our use of task-conditional module composition.

\paragraph{Hierarchical and Curriculum Learning.}
Hierarchical RL tackles long-horizon credit assignment and sparse reward signals. HAC \citep{Levy2017LearningHindsight} introduces layered actor-critic policies operating at different temporal resolutions. CAMRL \citep{Huang2023Curriculum-BasedLearning} proposes curriculum-driven asymmetric MTRL between teacher and student agents. Our method complements these paradigms by achieving curriculum-like generalization via unsupervised trajectory similarity and decentralized reuse.


\section{Extending MOSAIC to Lifelong Reinforcement Learning}
\label{sec:mask_composition_ll}

MOSAIC can be naturally extended to a lifelong learning (LL) setting \citep{delangecl2022survey,kudithipudi2022biological}, in which each agent learns a sequence of tasks while minimizing catastrophic forgetting and promoting forward transfer. LL MOSAIC agents can exploit their knowledge integration capabilities to accumulate knowledge that may derive from other agents, or from their own learning on previously encountered tasks. MOSAIC employs knowledge composition approaches that were developed in the field of lifelong reinforcement learning \citep{ben2022lifelong}. In the experimental settings for MOSAIC, however, we employed a one task per agent approach to maintain the focus on knowledge sharing and composition. This setting can be extended to a more general case in which one agent is not limited to learning one task. Therefore, MOSAIC can be easily adapted to a fully-fledged lifelong learning scenario by modifying the linear combination and \(\beta\) parameter adjustment.

Let agent \(i\) currently be learning task \(\tau_k+1\). Its mask is composed from three sources: the current task mask \(\phi_{\tau_k+1}\), prior task masks \(\{\phi_{\tau_k}\}_{k=1}^{K}\), and peer masks \(\{\phi_{j}\}_{j=1}^{n}\), acquired from other agents. The composed mask for task \(\tau_{k+1}\) for the forward pass is thus given by,
\begin{equation}
    \phi_{\tau_{k+1}}^\text{lc} = \beta_{\tau_{k+1}} \phi_{\tau_{k+1}} + \sum_{k=1}^k \beta_{\tau_k}  \phi_{\tau_k} + \sum_{j=1}^n \beta_j \phi_j,
\end{equation}
where each \(\beta\) is a scalar weight normalized via softmax. In this case, the initialization problem includes setting the weighting for previous task masks, the current task mask, and the newly acquired masks from other agents. One simple approach could simply be to extend Eq.\ \ref{eq:reward-guided-init} to 
\begin{equation}
    \beta_{\tau_{k+1}} = \frac{1}{3} + \frac{2}{3}r, \quad \beta_{\tau_k} = \frac{(1 - r)}{3k}, \quad \beta_j = \frac{(1 - r)}{3n}.
\end{equation}
The addition of lifelong learning presents an additional challenge. Boundaries in the sequence of tasks learned by an agent need to be identified, so that a new mask can be learned for each new task. One solution to this could be to use the cosine similarity computed from the Wasserstein embeddings to monitor the incoming data stream and decide when a new task starts using statistical methods \citep{dick2020detecting,dick2024statistical}.

\section{Additional Ablation Studies}
\label{sec:appendx_ablations}
Additional ablation studies 
An additional ablation study was conducted to assess the effect of communication frequency on MOSAIC’s performance in the image sequence learning benchmark. Specifically comparing frequencies of 1, 5, 10 (default), 25, and 40 iterations, where the frequency indicates how often agents query and integrate peer masks. As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:freq_ablation}, overly frequent communication (i.e., frequency of 1) results in performance degradation. In this setting, newly acquired peer masks continually override the agent's own beta parameter adjustments, preventing meaningful adaptation and fine-tuning outside of the performance-based adjustment. Frequencies of 5 and 10 yield comparable early performance, with a frequency of 10 achieving higher final return. Conversely, infrequent communication (25 or 40 iterations) reduces the opportunity for useful knowledge transfer, leading to weaker overall performance. These results highlight the importance of balancing between querying for peer knowledge often enough for a benefit to occur, and allowing agents to then tailor peer masks towards their own tasks.
